SCOPE: web/templates/components/whatif/budget-analysis.html, lines 33-40
(git diff master, +8 lines) — two new indented sub-rows under the Living
Expenses row: "Base (slider setting)" and "<Phase> phase ×<mult>". Standard:
/home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2/ACCESSIBILITY.md (numbered points cited below)
plus WCAG 2.2 AA.

Method: copied repo to a scratch worktree (git left untouched), added a
throwaway cmd/a11yrender/main.go that calls the real renderer
(internal/templates.NewFromFS + RenderToString) with the exact fixture from
the shipped test (internal/templates/render_budget_analysis_phase_subitems_
test.go), producing byte-real HTML for the new sub-rows. Wrapped that HTML in
a light-mode page and a `class="dark"` page, both loading the real built
web/static/css/tailwind.css (no synthetic markup, no hand-picked colors), and
ran `npx @axe-core/cli` (wcag2a/wcag2aa/wcag21aa/wcag22aa tags) against both.

1. CONTRAST (point 7, point 12) — FAIL, confirmed automated + manual.

   Panel background chain: budget-analysis.html:4
   `<div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 ...">` → light bg #ffffff,
   dark bg #1f2937 (Tailwind default gray-800; tailwind.config.js has no
   color overrides — `theme: { extend: {} }`).

   New sub-row markup (line 35-38):
     <span class="text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500">{{.Name}}</span>
     <span class="text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-300">...amount...</span>

   Rendered fixture output (Base/Go-Go sub-rows), verified in
   /tmp/.../scratchpad/panel.html:

   axe-core, light.html:
     Violation "color-contrast" (5 occurrences) includes:
       .pl-8.py-0\.5.flex:nth-child(3) > .text-gray-400.dark\:text-gray-500
       .pl-8.py-0\.5.flex:nth-child(4) > .text-gray-400.dark\:text-gray-500
     — these ARE the new "Base (slider setting)" and "Go-Go phase ×1.1"
     name spans.

   axe-core, dark.html:
     Violation "color-contrast" (4 occurrences) includes the same two
     selectors again.

   Manual WCAG ratio computation (sRGB relative-luminance formula) confirms
   and explains the axe result:
     - Name span, light: #9ca3af (gray-400) on #ffffff → 2.54:1
     - Name span, dark:  #6b7280 (gray-500) on #1f2937 → 3.04:1
     - Amount span, light: #6b7280 (gray-500) on #ffffff → 4.83:1 (passes)
     - Amount span, dark:  #d1d5db (gray-300) on #1f2937 → 9.96:1 (passes)

   Container is `text-sm` (line 19, `<div class="mb-4 text-sm">`) with no
   size/weight override on the sub-row — 14px normal weight, not "large
   text" under WCAG 1.4.3, so the 4.5:1 threshold applies, not 3:1. Both
   2.54:1 and 3.04:1 fail it. This fails in BOTH themes (not just one), so
   point 12's "parity" requirement is also violated — parity failing to hold
   in either theme is not compliance.

   PRE-EXISTING vs NEW: the failing class pair `text-gray-400
   dark:text-gray-500` is reused verbatim from this same file's existing tax
   sub-rows (e.g. "Includes State Tax" / "Includes NIIT" at lines 81/87/99,
   and the "Surplus"/"Additional withdrawal" captions at lines 114/122/153/
   214/225/245/249/260/262/271/277) — axe independently flagged two of those
   same pre-existing elements in both runs (`div:nth-child(1)/(2) >
   .text-gray-400.dark\:text-gray-500.text-xs`), confirming the underlying
   contrast defect already existed in this file before this diff and was not
   introduced by task W1. However, task W1 copied that already-failing class
   combination onto two brand-new text nodes ("Base (slider setting)",
   "<Phase> phase ×<mult>") that did not exist before this change. Per the
   audit brief, a pre-existing failure replicated into new content is still
   a reportable finding: this diff adds two more instances of an AA
   violation, and per the constitution's own words ("Applies to every page a
   task touches") a task that touches a panel and adds new failing text
   is not shielded by the defect already existing next door. RULING: FAIL,
   not a mechanical bypass of a known pre-existing bug — worker should reuse
   the passing pair already used one line up (`text-gray-500
   dark:text-gray-300`, 4.83:1 / 9.96:1) for the new Name spans instead of
   the failing pair, which would fix both the new instances without having
   to touch the older ones (those are out of scope for W1 but should be
   flagged separately for a dedicated contrast-sweep task).

2. STRUCTURE / semantics (point 2 n/a — no table here; general AT
   association) — PASS, with one observation.
   Rendered DOM (verified in panel.html) places both sub-row `<div>`s as
   immediate next-siblings of the "Living Expenses" row's `<div>`, before
   the next top-level breakdown item — genuine reading-order adjacency:
     <div class="flex justify-between py-0.5 pl-4 ">Living Expenses ...</div>
     <div class="flex justify-between py-0.5 pl-8">Base (slider setting)...
     <div class="flex justify-between py-0.5 pl-8">Go-Go phase ×1.1...
   The whole panel is div/flex-row based, not a table or list anywhere
   (including the pre-existing tax sub-rows), so no table/list semantics are
   broken by this change — consistent with the file's existing pattern.
   OBSERVATION (non-blocking): association is by visual indentation +
   DOM-order only, no `<dl>/<dt>/<dd>`, `role="group"`, or `aria-owns` tying
   sub-row to parent; a screen-reader user gets correct linear reading order
   but no explicit "this is a sub-item of Living Expenses" semantic. This is
   identical to the pre-existing tax sub-row pattern (not a regression
   introduced by W1), so it is not scored as a new failure.

   Sign character check (point 13, "no CSS-content text"): confirmed real
   text in both directions —
     - "+" comes from literal template text: budget-analysis.html:37
       `{{if and .SignedAmount (ge .Amount 0.0)}}+{{end}}`
     - "-" comes from formatMoney() (internal/templates/render.go:449-451),
       which returns the literal string "-$" + amount — not CSS
       `content:`, not `::before`. Screen readers will announce both.
       PASS.

3. MULTIPLIER CHARACTER — PASS.
   Source: internal/services/retirement/analysis/budget_fit.go:95
   `fmt.Sprintf("%s phase ×%s", phaseName, multiplierLabel)`. Byte-level
   check of the source file confirms the character is U+00D7 MULTIPLICATION
   SIGN (codepoint 0xD7 / decimal 215, i.e. the real multiplication glyph),
   not the ASCII letter "x". It is literal text inside the row's Name span,
   so it linearizes meaningfully for a screen reader, e.g. "Go-Go phase
   times 1.1" or equivalent AT pronunciation — not silent, not gibberish.

4. COLOR-ALONE CHECK (point 8) — PASS.
   The new amount span's color is static (`text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-300`)
   regardless of whether the adjustment is positive or negative — sign is
   conveyed by the literal "+"/"-" text (see #2), not by switching to
   red/green. No information in the new rows is color-only.

